\begin{quote}
\small
\noindent\textbf{Abstract.} Banks lend against assets that sit in specific places, and those places face changing climate hazards. This paper sets out a reproducible method, built entirely on open data, that links each loan in a portfolio to its exposure to extreme heat, drought, flooding and wildfire, and translates that exposure into adjusted probabilities of default and expected loss. The method is applied to a \emph{synthetic} portfolio of \VAR{n} loans spread across Tunisia's \VAR{ngov} governorates. Under SSP5-8.5, the share of exposure located in a high-hazard zone rises from \VAR{sh0}\,\% under the current climate to \VAR{sh5}\,\% by 2050 (range across climate models: \VAR{sh5lo}--\VAR{sh5hi}\,\%). The climate-adjusted extra expected loss reaches TND~\VAR{el5}~million, or \VAR{bp5}~basis points of exposure, TND~\VAR{vt5}~million more than under the current climate. Rain-fed cereals and olives are the most affected sectors. The elasticity linking hazard to default probability matters more for the result than the choice of climate model. All monetary figures are illustrative and describe no actual bank.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} physical climate risk, credit risk, CMIP6, NGFS, geoinformatics, Tunisia.
\end{quote}
\vspace{0.6em}

\section{Introduction}

Tunisian banks finance cereal and olive farms, food processors, textile workshops, coastal hotels, phosphate plants and housing. Each of these assets is exposed, in its own place and to its own degree, to heat, water scarcity, floods and fire. In 2022 the Basel Committee published eighteen principles for the management and supervision of climate-related financial risks \citep{bcbs2022}. In November 2026 the Central Bank of Tunisia launched a 2026--2030 roadmap on green finance and climate risk, following a technical assistance project with KfW.\footnote{As reported by the Tunisian press (Tustex, L'Économiste Maghrébin, La Presse, 4--5 November 2026); to be confirmed against the Central Bank's own publications.} Yet few institutions have a simple tool that ties each exposure to the geography of these hazards.

This paper proposes such a tool and documents the choices behind it. It asks three questions. What share of the loan book sits in a high-hazard zone today, in 2030 and in 2050? What adjustment to expected loss follows, under explicit assumptions? And which sectors and governorates carry that adjustment? The portfolio studied is entirely synthetic, in line with Tunisia's organic law no.~2004-63 on personal data protection, so the results speak to the method rather than to any institution.

Section~\ref{sec:framework} places the work in its regulatory context. Sections~\ref{sec:data} and~\ref{sec:method} describe the data and the method. Section~\ref{sec:results} presents the results, Section~\ref{sec:robustness} tests their robustness and Section~\ref{sec:limits} states their limits.

