The five CMIP6 models are ACCESS-CM2, CNRM-ESM2-1, MPI-ESM1-2-HR, MRI-ESM2-0 and IPSL-CM6A-LR. The reference period is 2010--2014, taken from each model's own historical run; each future horizon is a three-year window centred on 2030 and on 2050, under SSP2-4.5 and SSP5-8.5.

The synthetic portfolio holds \VAR{n} loans with total exposure of TND~\VAR{ead}~billion across ten sectors modelled on the structure of the Tunisian economy. Loans are placed according to population density, on cropland for agricultural borrowers and within a 3~km coastal strip for hotels; an automated check confirms that no loan falls at sea. Baseline probabilities of default range from 1 to 8\,\% by sector.

\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

For each loan $i$, hazard $h$ and scenario $s$, the physical indicator $x_{i,h,s}$ is mapped to a score between 0 and 1 using fixed bounds $a_h$ and $b_h$ that are the same for every scenario:
\begin{equation}
S_{i,h,s} = \min\!\left(1,\ \max\!\left(0,\ \frac{x_{i,h,s} - a_h}{b_h - a_h}\right)\right).
\end{equation}
Heat is measured by the number of days with maximum temperature above 35\,°C. Drought combines the longest dry spell with the change in annual rainfall relative to the same model's reference run. Flood uses the water depth of the 100-year event. Wildfire combines the share of burnable vegetation with a fire-weather term derived from the heat and drought scores.

The scores are combined into a composite weighted by the vulnerability $V_{k,h}$ of the borrower's sector $k$:
\begin{equation}
C_{i,s} = \frac{\sum_h V_{k(i),h}\, S_{i,h,s}}{\sum_h V_{k(i),h}}.
\end{equation}
The composite adjusts the probability of default, and flood depth adjusts loss given default for land and property collateral:
\begin{align}
PD_{i,s} &= \min\!\left(1,\ PD_i\,(1 + \beta_k\, C_{i,s})\right), \\
LGD_{i,s} &= \min\!\left(1,\ LGD_i\,(1 + \gamma\, S_{i,\text{flood},s})\right), \\
EL_{i,s} &= PD_{i,s}\, LGD_{i,s}\, EAD_i .
\end{align}
Extra expected loss is $EL_{i,s} - PD_i\,LGD_i\,EAD_i$. Because the baseline probability of default is treated as climate-neutral, this adjustment already exists under the current climate; the change relative to the current climate is therefore reported separately. A loan is in a high-hazard zone when at least one hazard material to its sector ($V_{k,h} \geq 0.5$) scores 0.6 or more. The parameters $\beta$, $\gamma$ and $V$ are illustrative assumptions (Appendix~\ref{app:assumptions}).

Climate uncertainty is represented by pairing each CMIP6 model with one member of the Aqueduct ensemble, which gives five realisations; results report the median and the range between the 10th and 90th percentiles.

