\section{Robustness}\label{sec:robustness}

Figure~\ref{fig:sens} shows how extra expected loss in 2050 under SSP5-8.5 responds to changes in the assumptions. Halving or doubling the elasticity $\beta$ moves the result by \VAR{bmin} to \VAR{bmax}\,\%, while the spread across climate models is only \VAR{mlo} to \VAR{mhi}\,\% around the median. A 25\,\% change in any vulnerability weight moves the result by less than \VAR{wmax}\,\%. One result looks odd at first: raising the flood weight slightly lowers the loss. Flood scores are low for most loans, so a heavier weight pulls the weighted average down. Calibrating $\beta$ on defaults observed after Tunisian droughts and floods would reduce uncertainty far more than adding climate models.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.42\textwidth}\centering\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig_sensitivity.pdf}\\[-0.2em]{\small (a) Sensitivity}\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.56\textwidth}\centering\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig_physrisk.pdf}\\[-0.2em]{\small (b) Comparison with OS-Climate physrisk}\end{minipage}
\caption{(a) Change in extra expected loss (SSP5-8.5, 2050) when one assumption changes. (b) ClimaRisk and physrisk indicators at the same locations in 2050: heat (ACCESS-CM2 model) and drought (multi-model ensemble).}\label{fig:sens}
\end{figure}

The indicators were also compared with the hazard indicators published by the OS-Climate \emph{physrisk} library at the same locations. For heat, the rankings agree (Spearman correlation of \VAR{rho_h0} under the current climate and \VAR{rho_h5} in 2050), although the levels differ: physrisk counts days on which the daily \emph{mean} temperature exceeds 35\,°C, which is far rarer. For drought, both approaches show conditions worsening everywhere, but their rankings are opposed (\VAR{rho_d5}): the SPEI index used by physrisk is normalised by local climate and highlights the wetter north, whereas dry-spell length is absolute and highlights the arid south. The choice of indicator should follow the activity being financed.

\section{Limits}\label{sec:limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item The portfolio is synthetic. Sector shares, probabilities of default and loss given default are documented assumptions, not central bank statistics.
\item The 0.25° climate grid (about 25~km) assigns each loan the climate of its grid cell; flood maps at about 1~km capture neither urban run-off nor small wadis.
\item Five models and three years per horizon cannot separate trend from variability for 2030; an operational version would use twenty-year windows and more models.
\item Aqueduct projections rest on CMIP5 and an observation-driven baseline, so the change in flood hazard over time is not like-for-like, and the pairing of models is arbitrary.
\item $\beta$, $\gamma$ and the vulnerability matrix are not estimated.
\item Only physical risk is covered: no transition risk and no second-round effects.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

With open data and a transparent method, each loan in a portfolio can be linked to its exposure to physical hazards and summarised in indicators a risk committee can read. In the synthetic portfolio studied here, heat raises exposure across the whole book, while drought concentrates the extra loss on rain-fed agriculture in the interior. The main source of uncertainty is not the climate but the translation of hazard into default probability, which will need to be calibrated on real bank data. Natural uses for the tool are materiality mapping, exploratory scenario analysis within the ICAAP \citep{ecb2020,eba2025}, and preparation for the Central Bank of Tunisia's forthcoming expectations.

